  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 409 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThere's an impressive bowling ball upper low over southern \nSaskatchewan and Manitoba this afternoon, which is driving a \ncold across western WI and far southeast MN. Our position south \nof this upper low puts us in its zone of subsidence and mid-\nlevel dry air, which means we get yet another dry cold front, \nwhich seems par for the course for an August that will see MSP \nfinish the month with just 0.46\" of rain (we average 4.34\"), \nwhich is the 4th driest August on record and just the 8th out of\n156 years where the Twin Cities failed to get at least 1\" of \nrain in the month of August. With that said, we can't completely\nrule out rain in the MPX area this evening, but that chance is \nin Eau Claire and Chippewa counties, to the east of Chippewa \nFalls and Eau Claire. East of the cold front, it's oppressive \nfor the last day of August, with temperatures in the upper 80s, \ndewpoints in the mid 70s, and heat index values in the mid 90s. \nAll of this heat and humidity is translating into instability, \nwith a pool of 3000+ j/kg of mlCAPE forecast develop ahead of \nthe front. Although isolated storm chances look better east of \nthe MPX area, the HRRR has been initiating storms in eastern Eau\nClaire county around 23z from the 15z run through its most \ncurrent run (19z), so we will have to keep an eye on the storm \npotential out around Stanley and Augusta. After that, the rest \nof the night will be quiet, with drier air moving in limiting \nthe fog potential in MN (where dewpoints are in the mid 50s \nacross central MN). WI will still have radiational fog potential\nas they will hold on to dewpoints in the 60s through the night.\n\nThis drier airmass moving in today will stay with us through the\nday on Tuesday, with dewpoints in the upper 40s to lower 60s \nmaking highs Tuesday in the 80s more tolerable. \n\nTuesday night is when things start to turn more active as a  \nshortwave coming out of the southwest monsoon travels across \nsouthern MN. Most guidance shows storms developing on the nose \nof the LLJ and riding the elevated CAPE gradient east Tuesday \nnight. Looking at the HREF/REFS, the pool of high muCAPE (over \n3000 j/kg) will remain near/north of the I-80 corridor in Iowa, \nwith the LLJ nosing up into northern Iowa. This all points to \nthe MN/IA border being the zone most conducive to seeing \nthunderstorms/heavy rain, with a lighter overrunning \nprecipitation area getting about as far north as the Twin Cities\nmetro, which will likely mean central MN into northwest WI \nmisses out on this one. Based on where the large scale forcing \nis forecast to be, the NBM this morning looked to have displaced\nit's higher PoPs/QPF a bit too far north, so did nudge PoPs a \nbit south Tuesday night to get closer to where the forcing is. \n\nWednesday through Labor Day begins a period of high confidence\nin summer-like warmth and humidity for the first week of \nmeteorological fall, but low confidence on precipitation. \nFiguring out severe weather potential comes down to figuring out\nwhere moisture (for said storms), forcing (something to provide\nlarge scale ascent for that moist air), instability (so that \nthe ascending moist air goes up in a convective updraft), and \nwind shear (let's organize those storms!) all come together. The\nlarge scale weather pattern through the weekend will become \namplified as a trough digs into the Pac NW. As it digs into the \nPac NW, we see the ridge over the central CONUS amplify, with a \nwesterly to southwest flow developing aloft locally. This setup \nwill pump deep moisture into our region from the Gulf and \nsouthwest Monsoon. Looking at the 4 ingredients, we'll have \nplenty of moisture (mean EPS Pwats are 150% to 200% of normal \nthrough the weekend), given the heat and humidity, instability \nwill be ample, and with the upper jet always overhead/nearby, \nwe'll have ample wind shear as well. The big uncertainty through\nthe weekend is the forcing variable. With the building upper \nridge, forcing looks subtle, with precipitation chances tied to \nwhere the warm front is on a daily basis and where any weak \nwaves move through in the upper flow. In other words, it's \nmesoscale features that will be driving our precipitations, \nwhich is hard to parse out more than 48 hours in advance. The \nresult is what you get with this forecast, numerous low chances \nfor precipitation through the holiday weekend. At this point, \nabout the best we can say is whatever we get, it looks to be \npredominately nocturnal in nature. Will we manage to finally \ncash in on a potential active weather pattern? Maybe, but when \nit comes to forecasting precipitation in a drought, I like to \nlive be the maxim of when in drought, leave it out.",
